  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows a ridge on the east side of the\nRockies and moving east into the Northern Great Plains. This\nwill keep much of the area under quiet and pleasant conditions.\nA change in weather will occur later tonight. A low pressure\ncenter, currently located over southeastern Washington, will\nbring high chances for rain showers and increase winds. \n\nRain: The latest 12Z model convective allowing models have rain\nstarting as early as 1 AM MDT / 2 AM CDT, from the southwest \nand expanding northeast through the rest of the morning. 12Z\nHREF has over 75% chances for greater than half inch for the\nevent from Highway 52 and northeast. Chances for a quarter of \nan inch or greater is 50% or higher from the Bismarck to \nWilliston and northeast. The southwest corner of the state has \nlowest chances to receive anything above a tenth of inch, but a \nfew model members hint at a rumble or two which can add a little\nmore rain accumulation. Rain chances will diminish by the \nmidday to early afternoon, clearing from west to east. \n\nWinds: At first, winds will predominately be from the \nsoutheast today (in the southwest) and widespread tonight with \ngusts up to 30 to 35 mph; the strongest winds will be in the \nsouthwest portion of the state. As the low pressure center gets \ncloser, the pressure gradient will increase across much of the \nstate and switching the wind direction from the southeast to \nmore westerly to northwesterly. Winds will gust high as 50 to 60\nmph by the afternoon. As result, there is a Wind Advisory in \neffect starting at 12 PM CDT /11 AM MDT for the western half of \nthe area. The central and east will start 3 PM CDT. Winds will \nstart to relax by late Monday night. \n\nBeyond Monday: Models show several weather disturbances passing\nover the area, bringing chances for rain showers. The synoptic \npattern will bring a slight warming due to minor ridging.",
